===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|Giapan||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|Japonia||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|Japon||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
First attested in English as {{m|en|Giapan}} in Richard Willes's 1577 ''The History of Travayle in the West and East Indies'' (cited in {{w|Peter C. Mancall}}'s ''Travel Narratives from the Age of Discovery'', pp. 156–57), translating a 19 February 1565 letter of the Portuguese Jesuit missionary {{w|Luís Fróis}} as "Of the Ilande of Giapan".

{{bor+|en|pt|Japão}} with possible influence from {{bor|en|nl|Japan}}, both from either or both:
* {{der|en|ms|Jepun}}, from {{der|en|nan-hbl|日本|tr=Ji̍t-pún}}, from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}.
* {{cog|id|-}} {{der|en|ms|Jepang}}, from {{der|en|nan-tws|日本|tr=Ji̍k-púng}}, from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}.
With {{IPAchar|/j/}} readings, such as {{m|en|Iaponia}} / {{m|en|Japonia}} or {{m|en|Japon}} / {{m|en|Iapon}} from {{bor|en|yue|日本}}, also from {{der|en|ltc|日本||sun origin}}. 

Compare also modern {{cog|cmn|日本}}, {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|日本|^にっぽ​ん}} / {{ja-r|日本|^にほ​ん}}, {{cog|ko|^일본}} ({{m|ko|日本}}), {{cog|vi|Nhật Bản}} ({{m|vi|日本}}).

The earliest form of ''Japan'' in Europe was {{w|Marco Polo}}'s {{m|it|Cipangu}}, from some form of synonymous {{der|en|zhx|-}} {{m|zh|日本國||nation of Japan}}.

